course: dictionary
lesson: 13812
title: A careful word for possible cheating
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words are chosen carefully so that nobody is blamed too soon. «The auditors found irregularities in the club's accounts.» Here, irregularities does not say someone stole the money. It says something may be wrong and needs checking.
explain: The ordinary meaning is things that are not even, regular or normal. «The road had irregularities, with bumps and holes all along it.» The bumps and holes are the clue that the word means uneven, not cheating.
explain: To work out which meaning is used, read the clues around the word. Accounts, auditors, votes and exams point to cheating or wrongdoing. Bumps, holes and surfaces point to uneven things. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it makes sense.
fact: Irregularities is a plural noun. One problem of this kind is «an irregularity».
fact: In official reports and the news, «irregularities» means possible fraud, cheating or wrongdoing, especially with money, votes or exams.
fact: This is a euphemism: a softer word used instead of a harsh one, so that nobody is accused before the facts are known.
fact: Literally, «irregularities» means things that are not even, regular or normal, such as «an irregularity in a surface» or «an irregularity in a heartbeat».
fact: Using this word does not prove that anyone is guilty. It means something may be wrong and it needs to be looked into.
exercise: «The officials found irregularities in the vote count.» What does irregularities most likely mean here? | Very long, boring lists of numbers | Bumps and holes in the ballot box | *Possible cheating or wrongdoing | Happy surprises
exercise: «The road had irregularities, with bumps and holes all along it.» What does irregularities mean here? | *Things that are not even or normal | Heavy rain | Lessons at school | Cheating with money
exercise: «The police are looking into irregularities in the exam results.» Why might the news use this word? | To say the police are happy with the results | To say the results are a joke | To say the exam was very easy | *To point to possible cheating without accusing anyone yet
test: «The auditor noticed irregularities in the club's money, but no one has been accused yet.» What does this suggest? | The auditor is ill and has gone home | The club has too much money | Everyone has already been found guilty | *Something may be wrong with the money, and it is being checked
test: «The doctor listened and found an irregularity in the heartbeat.» What does irregularity mean here? | *Not regular or steady | A kind of medicine | A very loud sound | Cheating in a test
exam: «The report listed irregularities in the election, but it did not name anyone.» Why does the report use this word? | It says the election was perfectly normal | It says everyone won the election | *It hints at possible cheating without blaming anyone yet | It says the votes were counted too quickly
